    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d426">

                <pb facs="0859" n="825" xml:id="pg825"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">695A.0024/3–3053</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Assistant Secretary of State for Far
                        Eastern Affairs (Allison)
                        to the Secretary of State</hi><anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d426-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1953-03-30">March 30, 1953</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>Subject:</label>
                    <item>Chou En-lai’s proposal for turning
                            POWs resisting repatriation over to a
                        neutral State<anchor n="2" xml:id="d426-f2"/></item>
                </list>
                <p>In connection with our consideration of this proposal, I think it interesting to
                    recall that General Clark made a very
                    similar suggestion in July, 1952. At that time he suggested that “upon signing
                    the armistice the supervision, control, and responsibility for determination as
                    to the ultimate disposition of all POWs of both
                    sides who have not been previously repatriated or released would be passed to a
                    group of neutral nations,” both sides to abide by the decision of such group for
                    disposition of non-repatriates.</p>
                <p>Similar suggestions were also at various times informally considered within the
                    Department and the Department of Defense but dismissed at the time because of
                    the practical difficulties. However, some of these practical difficulties are,
                    of course, lessened if there is agreement by both sides to such procedure. We
                    are urgently working on all aspects of this matter and the foregoing is for
                    background information only.</p>
                <p>The full text of that portion of General
                        Clark’s telegram referred to is attached.<anchor n="3"
                        xml:id="d426-f3"/></p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d426-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d426-f1-content">
                <p>A marginal note by O’Connor indicated
                    that the Secretary of State saw this memorandum on Apr. 4, 1953. It was drafted
                    by Johnson.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d426-f2" n="2" xml:id="d426-f2-content">
                <p>See the editorial note, <ref target="#pg824">p. 824</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d426-f3" n="3" xml:id="d426-f3-content">
